    "## 2.4. Comparison of Recording-Level and Subject-Independent Results\n",
    "\n",
    "This section compares the procedural reproduction of the official StressID recording-level evaluation with the subject-independent GroupKFold extension. Both protocols use the same handcrafted feature representation, classifier configurations, preprocessing operations, training-only SMOTE resampling, and evaluation metrics. The principal difference is therefore the partitioning strategy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The recording-level protocol uses ten random 80/20 train–test splits and may place recordings from the same participant in both partitions. The subject-independent protocol instead assigns complete participants to folds, ensuring that every test speaker is unseen during training. The comparison consequently estimates how model performance changes when speaker overlap is removed.\n",
    "\n",
    "For each classifier, the table reports the mean and sample standard deviation of weighted F1-score and balanced accuracy under both protocols, together with the absolute difference between the subject-independent and recording-level means. Negative differences indicate a performance reduction under the stricter subject-independent evaluation."
   ]
