    "## 2.5. XGBoost Hyperparameter Optimization with Optuna\n",
    "\n",
    "This section performs hyperparameter optimization for an XGBoost classifier trained on the 140-dimensional handcrafted representation. Each Optuna trial samples a tree-based configuration and evaluates it by cross-validation using mean weighted F1-score as the optimization objective. Class imbalance is addressed separately within each training fold through `scale_pos_weight`, calculated from that fold’s class distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "The default optimization scheme is subject-independent GroupKFold, so recordings from the same participant are never shared between the training and validation partitions of a trial. This aligns hyperparameter selection with the unseen-speaker setting examined in Section 2.3.2. A stratified recording-level scheme remains available only as an optional comparison.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because repeated optimization is computationally expensive, the trial history and selected hyperparameters are cached. Local execution allows the user to reuse a compatible cache or rerun the study, whereas Colab requires compatible cached results. Cache metadata records the dataset fingerprint, cross-validation scheme, search-space definition, number of trials, random seed, and software configuration relevant to the experiment.\n",
    "\n",
    "The best cross-validated Optuna score is used only for hyperparameter selection and is not interpreted as an unbiased final performance estimate. Final XGBoost performance must be assessed separately under an evaluation procedure that accounts for the preceding optimization stage."
   ]
  },
