   "source": [
    "## 2.7. Feature Importance Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "This section examines which handcrafted acoustic descriptors contribute most strongly to the tuned XGBoost classifier. The 140 feature dimensions are mapped back to interpretable descriptor names and broader acoustic families.\n",
    "\n",
    "Feature importance is estimated independently within each cross-validation training fold using XGBoost gain importance, which measures the average improvement in the objective produced by splits involving a feature. The same cross-validation scheme used for hyperparameter optimization is retained so that the interpretation remains aligned with the selected model configuration. For subject-independent GroupKFold, each fitted model is therefore trained without access to the participants assigned to the corresponding held-out fold.\n",
    "\n",
    "Individual-feature importances are averaged across folds and summarized by their mean and standard deviation. Family-level importance is obtained by summing the mean gain contributions of all descriptors belonging to each acoustic family and normalizing these sums to express their relative contribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "This analysis is descriptive rather than an additional benchmark. Feature importance indicates how the trained boosted-tree models use the available descriptors, but it does not establish that highly ranked features have a causal relationship with perceived stress."
   ]
